Scratch, local only.  Not part of the build and not meant to be merged.

What is here
------------
run-in-kvm.sh   launches the nested-KVM stand: vng + qemu-system-x86_64 with
                -cpu max, /tmp passed through rw, and kvm_amd loaded inside.
                Everything else is run as  bash run-in-kvm.sh <script>.
runq*.sh        one queued job each: build, then a run, then a grep of the log.
sc-*.sh         Strike Commander stands.  The interesting ones:
                  sc-gdb3.sh  watches the "VCPI: switch to PM" counter and,
                              when it stops growing, attaches gdb and dumps
                              every thread's backtrace.  Look for dosemu2.bin,
                              not bin/dosemu (that one is a shell wrapper).
                  sc-hma.sh   same, plus DOS=HIGH through
                              ~/.dosemu/drive_c/userhook.sys, to run the game
                              with HMA actually taken.
                  sc-fly5.sh  clicks PLAY, then Escape through the intro and
                              clicks TRAINING MISSION.
probe*.asm      tiny .COM programs built with nasm:
                  probe3  dumps the int 1 / int 3 vectors and the bytes at
                          the handler
                  probe4  dumps f000:0000 and f800:0000
                  probe5  XMS entry, then two "allocate HMA" requests
sc-rc-emu       dosemurc for the plain cpuemu runs of the probes.

The kvm.c commit on this branch is the probe itself: page-table walking
helpers over physaddr_to_unixaddr(), the XREG/XEIP/XEXIT samplers, the XHMA
watch on ffff:0010 and the XPR counters on the four early returns of
kvm_post_run().

Added later the same day
------------------------
The counters that found the wedge live in kvm.c on this branch:
  XPR     one line per 500 early returns of kvm_post_run(), one counter per
          reason, so a log that goes quiet says which of the four is looping
  XEXC    the full picture at the wedge: exception number, error code,
          ready_for_interrupt_injection, pending signals and a fresh
          KVM_GET_REGS
  XLOOP   every 50000 turns of the kvm_run() loop
  XEINTR  every 20000 EINTR retries that kvm_post_run() refused to return on
What they said: the wedge is kvm_post_run() answering "not yet" forever on a
pending #GP, and the registers at that moment are the host kernel's own
(rip=ffffffffc06031fa), so it is the nested KVM of the stand, not dosemu.

  mcal.sh       mouse calibration: probe6 samples int33 AX=3 ten times a
                second while known motions are injected.  Result: the guest
                cursor follows the X pointer position, scaled, and a plain
                click reaches the game.
  probe6.asm    that sampler
  xrel.c xnud.c xsweep.c
                relative-motion helpers, kept for the record; a plain
                absolute xclick turned out to be the right tool
  sc-fly8..11.sh
                stands that recognise the game's own menu by a crop of the
                frame before pressing anything, because the menu is only up
                for part of the attract cycle
  sc-fly9.sh    flight through the MENU.EXE stub with the HMA occupied: the
                run that reached the cockpit, took off and ended on the
                game's "YOU HAVE DIED" box
